<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kommos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kommos is a Minoan harbour town on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, at the edge of the Mesara plain, which probably served as the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a>. Excavated by Joseph Shaw from 1976, it has large stone buildings, ship sheds and imported pottery from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and later a Greek sanctuary used by Phoenician sailors.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harbour towns such as <b>Kommos</b>, probably the port of Phaistos, linked the island to the sea.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Nuragic pottery of the 13th century BCE has been found at Tiryns, <b>Kommos</b>, Hala Sultan Tekke and in Sicily, and pottery like it at Marsa Matruh in Egypt; a 2013 study found that most of the copper of Bronze Age Scandinavia came from Sardinia and Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Phaistos controlled the agricultural wealth of the Mesara and probably the nearby port of <b>Kommos</b>, which traded with Egypt and the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/state/palace-state-of-phaistos">Palace State of Phaistos</a></li></ul></section>
